    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d46">

                <pb facs="0112" n="30" sameAs="frus:frus1945Malta/pg30"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the President
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d46-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-08">8 January 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Personal and Top Secret for the President from Harriman.</p>
                <p>I have communicated to Marshal Stalin through Molotov the date you have selected
                    for the meeting, suggested his extending an invitation to the Prime Minister,
                    and submitted the code name you proposed. The above refers to White House cables
                    Nr 159<anchor n="2" xml:id="d46-f2"/> and 160<anchor n="3" xml:id="d46-f3"/>
                    January 6 and 7. I have arranged to bring down several Russian speaking American
                    officers to act as interpreters and to assist in dealing with the details of
                    talks with the Russians. I have already informed Molotov that there would be
                    about 100 in your party, which would take into account those coming from Moscow,
                    including General Deane, Admiral Olsen, General Hill, and the special <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">Milepost</hi> Planning Staff, General Roberts,
                    Captain Maples, USN, and Colonel Bogart With Molotov’s cordial approval I am
                    planning to go to the Crimea the end of the week just as soon as the Hungarian
                    Armistice <pb facs="0113" n="31" xml:id="pg31"/> is concluded and will then be
                    able to advise you in detail regarding the housing arrangements. The Russians
                    will no doubt provide a first class car for your personal use equipped with
                    bullet proof glass such as is used by Soviet high officials.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d46-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d46-f1-content">
                <p>Sent by the United States Naval Attaché, Moscow, via Navy channels.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d46-f2" n="2" xml:id="d46-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d46-f3" n="3" xml:id="d46-f3-content">
                <p>This message read as follows: “The British and American Chiefs of Staff have
                    agreed on the code name ‘<hi rendition="#small-caps">Argonaur</hi>’ for the
                    projected meeting” (Roosevelt Papers).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
